echo Committing (no-op if nothing changed)...
git commit -m "Three separate reasons a rule change never reached the output file. Together they are why nothing an analyst changed appeared in a download, why a Fix reported success and changed nothing, and why one analyst's change was invisible to a colleague. One: the download served STALE artifacts. download-all reuses the newest file on disk when regenerate is false and never once consulted the status field, even though a stale variable was declared right there and every write path had been dutifully marking outputs stale for weeks. Staleness was recorded everywhere and read nowhere. A stale artifact is now treated exactly like a missing one — fall through and rebuild — and the single-conversion download refuses with an explanation instead of handing back a file the rules have moved on from. That also explains the two-laptop symptom precisely: there is one shared database and one shared output directory, so both analysts were served the same cached file. Two: saving a rule never marked anything stale in the first place. The rule endpoints — add, edit, delete — wrote to the database and left the artifact flagged fresh, so even a correct reuse check would have kept the old file. Every write that changes what generation would produce now marks the outputs stale: add, update and delete rule, and approve and update mapping. Three, and this is why Batch ID kept shipping 900001 and Supplier Name New kept populating even though both are seeded as blank: the analyst's correction files were not feeding the strategy overlay's BLANK set. A suppress_field learning reaches the mappings, but the control-default pass skips a column only if it is named in that blank set, and _CONTROL_DEFAULTS carries batch id equals 900001. A field with no mapping row at all — which is exactly what an unmapped Batch ID is — was therefore refilled on every generate, by design, invisibly. The 30-Jul corrections now feed the overlay, so batch id, supplier name new, inactive date, tax reporting name, procurement bu and liability distribution are all in the blank set and nothing downstream can refill them. Alongside those: a Keep blank button, because 'leave this column empty' had no way to be said. Clearing a fixed value only leaves the field OPEN, which is not the same thing — a control default or the AI refills it on the next generate, which is precisely how Batch ID kept coming back. The button now clears the source and the default, marks the mapping not_applicable so the generator suppresses it, blanks any duplicate row for the same target field (not_applicable ranks BELOW approved in the per-target dedup, so a stale sibling would have won and shipped the value while the UI said blank — the same bug one layer down), writes a suppress_field learning so every current and future conversion inherits the decision, and marks the built outputs stale because the file on disk still has the value in it. Tax Organization Type is CORPORATION and INDIVIDUAL in Oracle's own casing, in both the corrections file and the strategy defaults, with a test that fails if the two ever disagree. And the answer to why Batch ID still read 'Defaulted to 900001' on screen after all of the above: two more layers were reporting a value nothing would write. The generated file was already blank; only the UI still said 900001, which is the worse of the two failures because it makes a correct fix look broken and invites re-fixing something that is not wrong. First, compute_effective_defaults — the endpoint the Mapping Review screen reads — had no suppression check at all. It skipped a field only when that field had a source column, so an unmapped, blanked Batch ID fell straight through to the control-defaults table and came back as 900001. It now excludes every field the analyst has said must ship blank, from all three sources the generator consults: the strategy corrections, suppress_field learnings, and not_applicable mappings. The check runs before the stored default_value is read, because a stale default left on the row by an earlier seed is exactly what kept re-appearing. Second, and this is the one no server-side fix could ever have reached: the Mapping Review page carried its OWN hard-coded copy of the control-defaults table, checked BEFORE the server's answer. No rule, learning, correction or Keep blank press can touch a constant compiled into the browser bundle. That copy had also drifted — it still said Tax Organization Type equals Corporation and Business Relationship equals PROSPECTIVE, both corrected on the server weeks earlier. It is deleted. There is now one source of truth for what a field will carry, and the endpoint returns the suppressed list alongside the defaults so a blanked field reads 'Left blank' rather than silently showing nothing. A mirror that cannot be corrected is worse than no mirror. One more thing that screenshot exposed: applies_to_all_sheets was already being written into the corrections file by the analyst, and NOTHING read it. It was dead data, so two rules that say 'all sheets' were applied to exactly one sheet. Batch ID -- 'Blank on ALL sheets, a batch identifier the loader assigns is not ours to invent' -- was filed under Supplier alone, so Supplier Site, Address, Site Assignment, Contact and Bank all kept shipping 900001 and kept saying 900001 on screen. Delivery Method's CASE_WHEN never reached Supplier Site, which is where the column actually lives. The flag is now honoured, matched by PREFIX of the object name rather than as a bare wildcard: a rule filed under Supplier covers every Supplier* sheet and stops there, because Customer has a Batch ID column too and silently extending an instruction past what was said is its own kind of bug. A sheet-specific rule still beats the bundle-wide one. And the biggest one, found by measuring the file the analyst sent back rather than by reading code: THREE fields their own corrections declare BLANK were populated on every row -- Supplier Name New on all 3,872 (carrying the supplier name, which asks Fusion to rename every supplier to the name it is being created with), Procurement BU on 5,315, Liability Distribution on 1,528. The corrections were correct and present. They were skipped because each field had a seeded source column whose mapping was APPROVED, and the overlay treats an approved mapping as the analyst overruling the rule. But approved is not proof a PERSON approved it: the learning engine approves the mappings it applies, under approved_by=learning-engine, and those rows were passing the guard. So a seeded mapping was outranking the analyst's own later correction -- the thing it was written to correct. The guard now requires a human approver, which is exactly the line the analyst drew: a person editing and approving in the UI outranks everything, an engine approval does not outrank the person who wrote the correction. Same test learning_service._eligible already had to make. A row with no recorded approver counts as not-a-person, because reading those as human approvals would restore the bug. Then three columns that were empty for three different reasons, all found by checking the rules against the 258 real headers of the NetSuite extract instead of against themselves. SUPPLIER SITE was blank on all 8,561 rows even though its rule is CONCAT of Country Code and City and BOTH columns exist: the generator prunes the source frame to the columns something claims and builds the per-row dict from the same set, and the overlay's own rules claimed nothing. Every strategy rule deriving from an unmapped column was evaluating against blanks and returning its default. The transformation-rule pipelines had been given this treatment; the overlay was added later and never inherited it, which is how this codebase has lost a guarantee before. The overlay now declares the columns its rules read. PARENT SUPPLIER was blank because SELF_LOOKUP reads its index from ctx self_index and NOTHING IN THE CODEBASE EVER BUILT ONE. It shipped, passed unit tests against a hand-made index, and returned its default on every row of every real run. The index is now built once per source frame before chunking, because a child's parent is usually in another chunk, and as a dict because 7,495 vendors scanned pairwise is 56 million comparisons. Columns match case-insensitively: the analyst wrote Internal Id, the extract says Internal ID, and losing the whole lookup to one letter is not a failure worth having. DELIVERY METHOD was blank because the rule looks for Remittance Email and Remittance Fax and the extract has no Remittance column at all - its headers carry Email, Alt. Email and Fax - and because the 13-Jul strategy's own narrower Supplier Site rule was shadowing the 30-Jul all-sheets one. Both are fixed, and directive_for now resolves ties by DATE. That is the analyst's precedence rule stated in full on 30-Jul: analyst manual change or the mapping file, whichever is latest, then learnings and golden records, then AI. Every rule file carries an effective date and every directive carries it, so a person's approval wins until the analyst issues a newer instruction in a file, at which point the file is the more recent statement of the same intent. A directive with no date loses to any human approval, which is the previous behaviour and the safe default for older files. Finally, a blank correction now CHANGES the library and the mappings rather than being added alongside them: the contradicting column_mapping rows are tombstoned and every existing conversion's mapping row is rewritten to not_applicable with source and default cleared. Seeding a suppression while leaving the old mapping alive left two rows saying opposite things about one field, and the older one kept winning. Mappings a person approved are left alone and counted as skipped_human rather than swallowed. Verified end to end against the real column spellings: Parent Supplier resolves to the parent's name, Supplier Site emits US-Chicago, Delivery Method emits EMAIL then FAX on every sheet. Then I tested the deployed instance through its own API instead of trusting the code, and it found three more. One: the blank enforcement was matching NOTHING live. It retired only column_mapping and example_default, while Tax Reporting Name -- declared blank -- still held a column_mapping from a gold example, a rule from a prior conversion AND an example_mapping, all binding it to Legal Name. It now retires every kind except the suppression itself, because listing kinds means the next kind someone adds silently reopens the hole. Two: the sweep was scoped to the correction's own client with an exact match, so the GLOBAL gold-example row survived -- and a global row is precisely what reaches this client, so the correction was recorded and then overruled by the thing it was meant to retire. Both the library sweep and the mapping rewrite now skip a row only when BOTH sides name a client and they differ; an unscoped row is not another tenant's. That is why nothing had been rewritten live: Supplier Name New was still suggested against New Vendor Approval and Tax Reporting Name still approved against Legal Name, on the very conversion the correction was written for. Three, and this one would have silently undone the whole day: a DERIVED field has no source column by nature and its mapping row is routinely not_applicable for exactly that reason -- and the generator skips a discarded column BEFORE the overlay block runs. Delivery Method is not_applicable on the live instance, so every fix made to that rule today would still have produced an empty column, and the same trap was already set for Supplier Site, Parent Supplier and Tax Organization Type. The directive is now resolved before the discard check and a rule or constant keeps the column alive; a BLANK directive still skips, because blank is what discarding means. A seam test asserts the ORDERING, since the ordering is the whole fix, and that only one lookup exists so the two cannot drift apart. Also added a reseed-supplier-corrections endpoint: these ran only at startup, which made every did-it-land question cost a redeploy and a cold boot, and it returns per-field enforcement counts so it-ran and it-changed-something stop looking identical. And the answer to why the tool was STILL following gold learnings: the library had no notion of when an instruction was given. Competing learnings for one field were ordered by TRANSFORM STRENGTH alone, so a gold example captured weeks earlier beat the mapping workbook the analyst had just handed over, and no date written anywhere could change that. Every learning now carries effective_date -- the date the INSTRUCTION was given, which is deliberately not captured_at: every startup seed stamps utcnow, so ordering on captured_at would make the 13-Jul strategy look newer than the 30-Jul corrections after any redeploy and the whole precedence would invert on a restart. Seeded rows take the _effective_date of the file they came from; rows captured from an analyst's own UI action leave it null and fall back to captured_at, which for them IS the moment the instruction was given. Candidates are now sorted latest-first, with transform strength demoted to a same-day tie-break so the Taxpayer ID protection survives. The defaults pass is ordered the same way, where first-come was letting an older gold default hold a field the analyst had since re-specified. That completes the precedence the analyst stated: 1) analyst manual change or the mapping file, whichever is latest 2) learnings and golden records 3) AI. 512 tests over 42 files, run on the pinned pandas 2.2.3 and on 3.0.2."
echo Pushing...
git push origin main
